SUMMARY TABLE (Case 1):

Iteration    slow    fast    Action
---------    ----    ----    ------
0            1       1       Start
1            2       3       Move both
2            3       5       Move both
3            4       2       fast wraps
4            5       4       Move both
5            5       5       COLLISION → true


WHY THEY ALWAYS MEET IF LOOP EXISTS:

• Fast gains 1 step on slow each iteration (2 - 1 = 1)
• Inside a loop of size L, fast will eventually lap slow
• Distance between them decreases by 1 each turn
• They MUST meet within L iterations after both enter the loop


//////////////////////// QUESTION ////////////////////////////

Detect Loop in Linked List

Given the head of a singly linked list, determine whether the linked list contains a loop or not.

A loop exists if the last node's next pointer points to any node in the list (including itself) instead of null.

Examples:

Input:  1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 4 -> 5 (loop back to 2)
Output: true

Input:  1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 4 -> 5 -> null
Output: false

Input:  1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 1 (circular)
Output: true

Constraints:
- 1 ≤ number of nodes ≤ 10^5
- 1 ≤ node->data ≤ 10^3
- 0 ≤ pos ≤ number of nodes (pos = 0 means no loop)

Time Complexity: O(n) - fast pointer traverses at most 2n nodes
Space Complexity: O(1) - only 2 pointers used

Algorithm: Floyd's Cycle Detection (Tortoise and Hare)
